            <ArticleTitle>Closure of a penetrating ulcer of the descending aorta using an Amplatzer occluder.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e18-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2009.06.092</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Symptomatic patients with penetrating atherosclerotic ulcers of the descending thoracic aorta have traditionally been treated by using an open surgical repair. The emergence of thoracic aortic stent-graft technologies has introduced a less invasive treatment option, which often involves covering excess portions of a normal aorta with stent-graft material. We describe the mid-term follow-up of a patient with a symptomatic penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer of the descending thoracic aorta treated with an Amplatzer septal occluder device (AGA Medical Corp, Plymouth, MN), which is typically used for structural heart disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
